      "text": "First, we consider each part separately.\n\n1. Evaluate $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}}$:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{4}} = \\frac{3}{2} = 1.5\n\\]\nThus, $\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}}\\right\\rceil$ is the smallest integer greater than or equal to $1.5$, which is 2.\n\n2. Evaluate $\\frac{9}{4}$:\n\\[\n\\frac{9}{4} = 2.25\n\\]\nThe ceiling function here, $\\left\\lceil\\frac{9}{4}\\right\\rceil$, gives the smallest integer greater than or equal to $2.25$, which is 3.\n\n3. Evaluate $\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)^2$:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) = \\frac{81}{16} = 5.0625\n\\]\nSo, $\\left\\lceil\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)^2\\right\\rceil$ is the smallest integer greater than or equal to $5.0625$, which is 6.\n\nNow, we sum these values:\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}}\\right\\rceil + \\left\\lceil\\frac{9}{4}\\right\\rceil + \\left\\lceil\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)^2\\right\\rceil = 2 + 3 + 6 = 11\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]",
